Document the network path Spark needs, and the schedule contract

Two things were missing from the install guide, and both cost real time
to rediscover.

The gateway section only ever covered the backend's route to it, through
VNet integration. A Spark notebook cannot use that path: it runs in
Microsoft's own managed network, in another region, and cannot be placed
in our VNet. Without a Fabric managed private endpoint to the Container
Apps environment, the sync notebook fails on name resolution and Fabric
reports only "System cancelled the Spark session due to statement
execution failures", which names the symptom and not the cause.

The section now covers creating that endpoint, approving it, the
PrivateEndpointConnectionLockConflict that a too-early approval returns,
the lag between Azure reporting Approved and Fabric reporting Succeeded,
and the fact that it is per workspace.

The schedule contract is written down for the same reason: the workload
SDK's itemSchedule client is for custom item types, not first-party
notebooks, and the four details that decide whether the REST call is
accepted are not obvious from the schema.

WORKLOAD.md gains the rules behind Active, Planned and Warning, which
until now were words with no definition behind them.

